WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.834
A new report in The New

00:00:00.834 --> 00:00:01.634
York Times reveals

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:03.136
that Democratic Senator

00:00:03.136 --> 00:00:06.573
Bob Menendez, his wife Nadine, hit

00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:07.807
and killed a man

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:09.509
with her car in 2018

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:11.745
before she and the senator will married.

00:00:11.745 --> 00:00:13.413
And this car crash is allegedly

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:15.181
what started the bribe

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:15.982
that was listed

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:17.650
in last month's federal indictment

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:19.953
against Senator Menendez his wife

00:00:19.953 --> 00:00:20.920
and three others.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:22.155
The indictment claims

00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:23.590
that two of those co-defendants

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:24.090
offered to help

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:27.627
by name Dino Menendez a new Mercedes

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:28.762
in exchange

00:00:28.762 --> 00:00:29.562
for the senator

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:31.765
interfering in the prosecution

00:00:31.765 --> 00:00:33.199
of one of the co-defendants

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:34.300
business associates.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:35.301
Nadine Menendez,

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:36.336
his lawyer told The New York

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:37.537
Times, quote,

00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:39.806
My understanding was this individual ran

00:00:39.806 --> 00:00:40.807
in front of her car

00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:42.409
and she was not at fault, unquote.

00:00:42.409 --> 00:00:44.611
Joining us now is CNN's John Miller.

00:00:44.611 --> 00:00:45.645
John, thanks for joining us.

00:00:45.645 --> 00:00:47.013
So according to The Times, Mrs.

00:00:47.013 --> 00:00:49.082
Menendez was not tested

00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:51.117
for drinking or drugs after the crash.

00:00:51.584 --> 00:00:54.687
How unusual is it for police to not check

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:57.057
for that after a deadly car accident?

00:00:57.824 --> 00:00:59.859
Well, in the state of New Jersey,

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:02.662
to get a test like that in a motor

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:03.496
vehicle accident,

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:05.331
even one with a fatality,

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:07.434
there has to be not probable cause,

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:09.969
but at least reasonable suspicion

00:01:09.969 --> 00:01:11.237
observed by the officer

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:11.871
that the person

00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:13.807
might be in intoxicated condition

00:01:13.807 --> 00:01:14.741
or on drugs

00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:15.608
that would be slurred

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:17.310
speech, unsteady on her feet,

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.212
smell of alcohol on the breath

00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:20.914
and police don't observe

00:01:20.914 --> 00:01:22.248
any of that at the scene,

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:23.516
according to their report.

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:25.518
And when you look at the video,

00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:27.687
she is articulating the words clearly.

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:29.656
She appears to be steady.

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:31.591
So it's not something that

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:32.592
they could have done

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:34.194
absent one of those factors.

00:01:35.462 --> 00:01:38.264
Toward video, a retired police officer

00:01:38.665 --> 00:01:39.933
arriving on the scene

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:41.501
saying that his wife was friends

00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:43.636
with Miss Menendez

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:44.370
and was asking

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.239
about any potential charges.

00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:48.408
Does that raise your antenna at all?

00:01:49.375 --> 00:01:50.944
I mean, it would raise my antenna

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:52.245
just because it should.

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:54.347
But if you listen to at least

00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:56.149
what's recorded on tape,

00:01:56.149 --> 00:01:56.883
I think what's more

00:01:56.883 --> 00:01:57.650
significant is

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.386
what he's not asking what he's asking is.

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:03.957
He's a retired officer.

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:04.557
He's asking

00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:06.092
if the prosecutor's office

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:07.660
is going to be involved

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.829
in Bergen County, New Jersey.

00:02:09.829 --> 00:02:11.131
If there's a fatal accident,

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:12.365
the prosecutor's office

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:13.733
actually runs the team

00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:15.502
along with the sheriff's department

00:02:15.502 --> 00:02:16.769
that comes to the scene

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:18.771
of fatal accidents and investigates

00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:20.473
what is not asking

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:20.874
is he's

00:02:20.874 --> 00:02:22.375
not taking the officer on the side

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:24.811
and saying, is there anything we can do?

00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:25.678
Can you help us out?

00:02:25.678 --> 00:02:27.413
Is there a professional courtesy?

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:28.214
He's just asking

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:29.415
if the prosecutor's office

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:30.450
is going to be notified.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.584
And he's told, yes, it is.

00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:33.453
And he says, well, I understand why.

00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:35.121
Indicating that they understand

00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:36.990
that the victim has passed away.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.525
So Richard Koop was the individual

00:02:38.525 --> 00:02:39.726
killed in the accident

00:02:39.726 --> 00:02:42.262
and his family has lots of questions.

00:02:42.462 --> 00:02:43.329
Who makes the call on

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:45.031
whether an investigation

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:47.300
into a fatal car accident like this

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.502
stays open or is closed?

00:02:49.936 --> 00:02:51.838
Well, this happens in the township of

00:02:51.838 --> 00:02:53.506
but go to New Jersey

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:55.575
and their police department responds

00:02:55.575 --> 00:02:58.077
and they immediately control the scene

00:02:58.411 --> 00:03:00.213
But ultimately, the Bergen County

00:03:00.213 --> 00:03:01.581
prosecutor's office

00:03:01.581 --> 00:03:02.282
and the sheriff's

00:03:02.282 --> 00:03:03.850
bureau criminal investigation

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:06.085
take over the processing of the scene

00:03:06.085 --> 00:03:07.453
and make the determination

00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:09.589
on criminal charges in this case.

00:03:09.589 --> 00:03:11.391
There were no criminal charges brought.

00:03:11.391 --> 00:03:12.859
There was no summons issued

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.795
based on the video they recovered.

00:03:15.795 --> 00:03:16.896
And her statement,

00:03:16.896 --> 00:03:17.664
she said,

00:03:17.664 --> 00:03:19.199
I was driving along,

00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:21.868
the individual darted out between cars

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:23.303
and the next thing I know, you know,

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:25.338
he was on top of my windshield.

00:03:25.338 --> 00:03:26.539
That is roughly it.

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:28.174
Absent something to disprove

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:29.576
that, it would have been difficult

00:03:29.576 --> 00:03:32.011
for them to bring a criminal charge.

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:32.912
They issued a subpoena

00:03:32.912 --> 00:03:34.147
for her phone, though. And check.

00:03:34.147 --> 00:03:35.348
This is important.

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:36.783
That means was she texting

00:03:36.783 --> 00:03:38.818
when when she didn't see him?

00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:40.386
Was she talking on the phone?

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:41.955
Was she doing something else?

00:03:41.955 --> 00:03:44.157
And we don't have in any of these reports

00:03:44.157 --> 00:03:45.458
the results of that subpoena.

00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:46.593
So a couple of more questions

00:03:46.593 --> 00:03:47.393
we have to run down.

